## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `GetNextItem()` may block | Blocking call interruption | `token.WaitHandle` / async alternative |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to loop exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs and propagates through the call chain |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | The new exception type thrown by token-aware APIs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — loop naturally exits | No injected exception to suppress |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Actually waits for thread exit instead of hoping |

---

### Critical: Make `GetNextItem` Cancellation-Aware

The original `GetNextItem()` "may block" — this is the key pitfall. A cancelled token **won't interrupt** a blocking synchronous call unless you wire it in:

```csharp
// If using a blocking collection:
private object GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // BlockingCollection natively supports CancellationToken
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If using a WaitHandle/ManualResetEvent to signal new work:
private object GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(
        new[] { _itemAvailable, token.WaitHandle });

    if (index == 1)
        throw new OperationCanceledException(token);

    return DequeueItem();
}
```

---

### Common Pitfalls for This Migration

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | Thread hangs forever on `Stop()` — cancellation never reaches the blocking call |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Timer/callback leak if `CancelAfter` is used later |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated cancellations |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside it |
| **Replacing `Thread` with `Task.Run` without need** | The `Thread` usage here is fine in .NET 8 — only the abort pattern needed changing |

---

### Optional: Async Modernization (If Desired)

If you want to go fully async (not required for the abort migration):

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            }
            finally
            {
                await FlushBuffersAsync();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_cts is not null)
        {
            _cts.Cancel();
            if (_workerTask is not null)
                await _workerTask.ConfigureAwait(false); // clean exit
            _cts.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
```

This is **optional** — the synchronous `Thread` + `CancellationToken` version above is the correct minimal migration.
